{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-321074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t+1}{3},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-4)(1-t)^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-321074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{t+1}{3}\\,⇒\\,t=3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=3x-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((3x-1)^{2}-4){(1-(3x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x+1-4)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x-3)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3{x}^{2}-2x-1)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-321074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75efc63830d8979ff86c80e34ac25462cd9c6d21797238664c242c3f6d3bab4b"} {"question" "q-321074" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-321074" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "((3x-1)^2-4)(2-3x)^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-320871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t-2,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-4,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-320871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=t-2\\,⇒\\,t=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=x+2$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x+2)^{2}-4 \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y={x}^{2}+4x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-320871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75efc63830d8979ff86c80e34ac25462cd9c6d21797238664c242c3f6d3bab4b"} {"question" "q-320871" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-320871" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x^2+4x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t-2},\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\ne 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{t-2} \\\\ x(t-2) & =1 \\\\ t-2 & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{x}+2 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t=\\frac{1+2x}{x}$ into our expression for $y={t}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t-2},$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75efc63830d8979ff86c80e34ac25462cd9c6d21797238664c242c3f6d3bab4b"} {"question" "q-15678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-{(\\frac{-1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0"] ["math" "y=-{(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0"] ["math" "y={(\\frac{-1-2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0"] ["math" "y={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0"] ["math" "y={(\\frac{1-2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-6828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+5,\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,t\\ne 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-6828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for ${t}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x={t}^{2}+5\\,⇒\\,{t}^{2}=x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${t}^{2}=x-5$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x-5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{1}{x-5}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 0$ and $x={t}^{2}+5,$ we must have $x\\ne 5.$ \n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x-5},\\,x\\ne 5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-6828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75efc63830d8979ff86c80e34ac25462cd9c6d21797238664c242c3f6d3bab4b"} {"question" "q-6828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-6828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\frac{2}{x},\\,x\\ne 0"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{x+5},\\,x\\ne -5"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{x-5},\\,x\\ne 5"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0"] ["math" "y=\\frac{5}{x},\\,x\\ne 0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-6828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{1}{x-5},\\,x\\ne 5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-321141" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1},\\,\\,\\,y=(t+1)^{2},\\,t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-321141" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1}\\,⇒\\,2t+1=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}+1)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1+x}{2x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2t+1}$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-321141" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75efc63830d8979ff86c80e34ac25462cd9c6d21797238664c242c3f6d3bab4b"} {"question" "q-321141" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-321141" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\left(\\frac{1+x}{2x}\\right)^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-321141" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-321141" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75efc63830d8979ff86c80e34ac25462cd9c6d21797238664c242c3f6d3bab4b"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{x+4}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+1}{2})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{x+4}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-1}{2})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{x-1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+4}{5})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{x-1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75efc63830d8979ff86c80e34ac25462cd9c6d21797238664c242c3f6d3bab4b"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}=2\\pi "] ["math" "{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x+2 \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=y-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75efc63830d8979ff86c80e34ac25462cd9c6d21797238664c242c3f6d3bab4b"} {"question" "q-15679" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15679" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(y+3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=3"] ["math" "(y-2)^{2}+(x-3)^{2}=1"] ["math" "(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1"] ["math" "(y-3)^{2}+{x}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{y}^{2}+(x+3)^{2}=3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15679" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-102682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-102682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x-3}{5} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y+2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-102682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75efc63830d8979ff86c80e34ac25462cd9c6d21797238664c242c3f6d3bab4b"} {"question" "q-102682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-102682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-3}{3})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{x-3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{5})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{x-3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+5}{3})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{x-5}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+3}{2})}^{2}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-102682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$ by ${sin}^{2}⁡t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75efc63830d8979ff86c80e34ac25462cd9c6d21797238664c242c3f6d3bab4b"} {"question" "q-15761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{2}=1+{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{2}=1-3{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{2}=1-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "q-15761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "daed0ff373541b0c868c30b724fcbe470ff2aa15d85de26b18aa240b2aaac983"} {"question" "e-5087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/example-5087.json" "file_sha256" "d02d5462908fc31ade5802f05e9f152c19c0bd10eca79ad08fe8da5b947be78b"} {"question" "e-5087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1.$\n\nFinally, since $t \\geq0$ and $x=2t^2,$ we must have $x \\geq 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0 .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/example-5087.json" "file_sha256" "d02d5462908fc31ade5802f05e9f152c19c0bd10eca79ad08fe8da5b947be78b"} {"question" "e-5101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/example-5101.json" "file_sha256" "549f7e93a2493d8d64f787800604628daefd5042825f50b3c38b437595295283"} {"question" "e-5101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/example-5101.json" "file_sha256" "549f7e93a2493d8d64f787800604628daefd5042825f50b3c38b437595295283"}] "usage" {"q-15678" [] "e-5087" [] "q-15752" [] "e-5101" [] "q-15751" []} "basis" 1073 "retractions" [[17592186101123 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$x = \\frac{1}{t - 2}, y = t^{2}, t \\ne 2$."] [17592186101124 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$x = 2 \\sin   t - 1 , \\, \\, \\, y = 5 \\cos   t + 4 , \\, t \\in  [ 0 , 2 \\pi  ) .$"] [17592186101125 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$x = 2 \\cos   t , \\, \\, \\, y = 3 \\sin   t , \\, t \\in  [ 0 , 2 \\pi  ) .$"] [17592186100626 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n$x=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0$."] [17592186100626 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t$. First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2$:\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting\n\n$$\nt^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\ninto our expression for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= t^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\nt \\geq0\n$$\n\nand $x=2t^2$, we must have\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0\n$$"] [17592186100627 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n$x=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$."] [17592186100627 :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}x = \\csc   t \\, & \\Rightarrow  \\, \\csc   t = x \\\\ y = 2 \\cot   t \\, & \\Rightarrow  \\, \\cot   t = \\frac{y}{2} .\\end{array}$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1\n$$\n\nby $\\sin^{2}{t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sin^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t} + \\frac{\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t} &= \\frac{1}{\\sin^{2} t} \\\\\n1 + (\\frac{\\cos t}{\\sin t})^{2} &= (\\frac{1}{\\sin t})^{2} \\\\\n1 + \\cot^{2} t &= \\csc^{2} t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and\n\n$$\n\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}\n$$\n\ninto this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + (\\frac{y}{2})^{2} &= x^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} &= x^{2} - 1 \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4(x^{2} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is\n\n$$\ny^2 = 4\\left(x^2-1 \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nand $x=\\csc t$, the interval on the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186098509 17592186098510 17592186098511 17592186098512 17592186098513 17592186098515 17592186098516 17592186098517 17592186098518 17592186098519 17592186098521 17592186098522 17592186098523 17592186098524 17592186098525] "immutable_fields" [[17592186098508 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098508 :answer-field/id] [17592186098508 :answer-field/key] [17592186098508 :answer-field/type] [17592186098514 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098514 :answer-field/id] [17592186098514 :answer-field/key] [17592186098514 :answer-field/type] [17592186098520 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098520 :answer